#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521447 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "edd45b2c-b238-5306-8bee-cea5535e0e17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521510 #edb/ref 1071 "In a physics experiment, the net charge on a sensor (in $mC$) after $t$ seconds is given by the function $Q(t).$ The values of $Q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.0$ | $1.5$ | $2.0$ | $2.5$ | $3.0$ | $3.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$Q(t)$** | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n\nApproximate the sensor’s charge instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.5$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.5.$\n\nThe sensor’s charge at $t=2.5$ is decreasing at approximately \n{{field-1}} $mC$ per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6feb34ac-08d5-5bc5-ac07-3b8f7d9f2f78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521510 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{Q(t+h)-Q(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat time $t=2.5$ with step size $h=0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(2.5) & ≈\\frac{Q(2.5+0.5)-Q(2.5-0.5)}{2(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q(3.0)-Q(2.0)}{1.0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2}{1.0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{1.0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sensor’s charge at $t=2.5$ is decreasing at approximately $3\\,mC$ per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521517 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b7/b76466e577343a234b53566b00939a3de312ad4f71106ebf3cbed3bc9d8795ea.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=8$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7efd0b4a-5765-528c-b6d9-1a45ad254c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521517 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=8$ with step size $h=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(8) & ≈\\frac{f(8+4)-f(8-4)}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(12)-f(4)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-6}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521534 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4b/4b280f9756119d26317899e07e9a23b3679555701b23096516afb7a646cda453.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=5$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a46b6ba-aacd-5c29-a1ea-c98b8e558d24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521534 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=5$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{f(5+1)-f(5-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(6)-f(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521548 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0b/0bfc66c5e19ff0fe88cba465c6b045402fc5f8753befa4acb3b1cc3839acf8a7.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=2$ and $x=4.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd26c621-effc-5c15-8ae1-c10830f17e99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521548 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+1)-f(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521567 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521567 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521567 #edb/ref 1071 "The population growth of a developing rural area was studied for $25$ years. The following table shows the population $p(t),$ in hundreds, recorded $t$ years after $1990.$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(t)$ | $90$ | $98$ | $116$ | $138$ | $178$ | $220$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the population growth rate in the year $2005$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eee2d2d0-57b3-5f3a-92f2-640ee03bafc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521567 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{p(t+h)-p(t-h)}{2h}.\n$$\n\n \nWe note that year $2005$ corresponds to $t=15.$ The step size is $h=5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{p(15+5)-p(15-5)}{2(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p(20)-p(10)}{2(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{178-116}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{62}{10} \\\\  & =6.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the population growth rate in $2005$ was $620$ people per year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a14f9f1-1b0f-594d-a6d2-e0a52c7322ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "75e3868f-a25b-5151-8a0c-5be1318bc937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521620 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "837165f8-c967-55f3-b235-24e13a193e14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521639 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c47052fd-5450-5904-80f3-546487c65903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521639 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef594372-6cb5-54ca-9621-701e5b7ba954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521517 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
